/* Bassiet book, Depth 11: The Villous Forest. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c11-gut']={depth:11,title:'The Swaying Forest',
epi:'Fingers of tissue sway in a current that has stopped. They are starving, surrounded by nothing.',
c:['peptic-ulcer','ibd','malabsorption','gi-hormones','g-proteins-toxins','b12-folate','vitamins'],
S:[
{h:'Signals that run the gut',p:[
'The gut obeys three layers of command. The **enteric nervous system** can run it alone: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the muscle layers drives motility, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus drives secretion and local blood flow. The **vagus** adds parasympathetic drive (acetylcholine raises secretion and motility, and vagal fibres release GRP onto G cells), while sympathetic fibres from the coeliac and mesenteric ganglia slow the tube and close its sphincters. Over both sit the **hormones**, released by scattered enteroendocrine cells that taste what reaches the lumen.',
'Read the hormone table as a conversation along the tube. Food in the stomach calls for acid (gastrin). Acid arriving in the duodenum calls for bicarbonate and for the stomach to hold back (secretin). Fat and protein in the duodenum call for bile and enzymes and slow gastric emptying further (CCK). Glucose in the small bowel warns the islets before it reaches the blood (GIP and GLP-1, the incretins), which is why oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose given intravenously.'],
table:{cols:['Hormone','Source','Stimulus','Actions'],rows:[
['Gastrin','G cells, antrum','peptides, distension, vagus (GRP); inhibited by acid via somatostatin','↑acid, gastric mucosal growth; very high in Zollinger-Ellison, on PPIs and in atrophic gastritis'],
['Somatostatin','D cells','acid','inhibits almost everything; octreotide treats variceal bleeding, acromegaly, carcinoid'],
['CCK','I cells, duodenum and jejunum','fat, protein','gallbladder contraction, pancreatic enzymes, relaxes sphincter of Oddi, slows gastric emptying'],
['Secretin','S cells, duodenum','acid, fatty acids','pancreatic and biliary HCO₃⁻, ↓gastric acid; paradoxically raises gastrin from a gastrinoma (secretin stimulation test)'],
['GIP','K cells','fat, protein, glucose','↑insulin (incretin), ↓acid'],
['GLP-1','L cells, ileum and colon','food','↑insulin, ↓glucagon, slows emptying, satiety'],
['Motilin','small intestine','fasting','migrating motor complex; erythromycin mimics it'],
['Ghrelin','stomach (fundus)','fasting','hunger, GH release; very high in Prader-Willi syndrome'],
['VIP','neurons','distension, vagus','↑water and electrolyte secretion, relaxes smooth muscle; VIPoma: watery diarrhoea, low K⁺, achlorhydria']]},
s:[['phys','Parietal cells secrete H⁺ through the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, driven by gastrin (mostly indirectly, via histamine from ECL cells), histamine (H₂, cAMP) and acetylcholine (M₃, Ca²⁺). For every H⁺ secreted a HCO₃⁻ leaves into the blood, the alkaline tide after a meal. Parietal cells also make intrinsic factor; chief cells make pepsinogen and gastric lipase.'],['pharm','Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole) irreversibly block the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, the final common step, so they suppress acid whatever the stimulus. Long-term use raises the risk of C. difficile infection, pneumonia, fractures, low Mg²⁺ and low B12. H₂ blockers (famotidine) are weaker; cimetidine also inhibits CYP450 and is anti-androgenic (gynaecomastia).']]},
{h:'Digestion and absorption',p:[
'Carbohydrates: salivary and pancreatic amylase, then brush-border disaccharidases; glucose and galactose enter by **SGLT1** (with Na⁺), fructose by **GLUT5**; all leave by GLUT2. Lactase deficiency: bloating and osmotic diarrhoea after milk (low stool pH, positive hydrogen breath test, normal mucosa). Proteins: pepsin, then trypsin (activated by enterokinase), chymotrypsin and carboxypeptidase; absorbed as amino acids and di- and tripeptides. Fats: emulsified by bile salts, cut by pancreatic lipase, carried as micelles to the brush border, rebuilt into triglyceride and packaged with apoB-48 into chylomicrons, which enter the lacteals.',
'Where things are absorbed: **iron** in the duodenum (as Fe²⁺), **folate** in the jejunum, **B12** and bile salts in the **terminal ileum**. Bile salts are reabsorbed and recycled (enterohepatic circulation), so losing the terminal ileum causes fat malabsorption, B12 deficiency, **calcium oxalate kidney stones** (unabsorbed fat binds calcium in the gut, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed) and **cholesterol gallstones** (a smaller bile salt pool).',
'To find the broken step, ask whether the mucosa or the enzymes have failed. **D-xylose** is absorbed by the proximal small bowel without pancreatic help: low urine and blood xylose means mucosal disease (coeliac, Whipple, bacterial overgrowth); a normal result with steatorrhoea points to pancreatic insufficiency. Low faecal elastase confirms the pancreas.'],
table:{cols:['Malabsorption','Clues'],rows:[
['Coeliac disease','gluten (gliadin) deamidated by tissue transglutaminase and shown on HLA-DQ2/DQ8; anti-tTG IgA (check total IgA; IgA deficiency gives false negatives); villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes, mostly duodenum; dermatitis herpetiformis; iron deficiency; enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma; gluten-free diet'],
['Tropical sprue','residents of or visitors to the tropics; whole small bowel; responds to antibiotics and folate'],
['Whipple disease','Tropheryma whipplei; PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the lamina propria; older men; arthralgia, diarrhoea, cardiac and neurological signs; long antibiotic courses'],
['Bacterial overgrowth','stasis (blind loop, strictures, scleroderma, diabetic neuropathy); bacteria deconjugate bile salts and consume B12; low B12 with normal or high folate (bacteria make folate); antibiotics'],
['Pancreatic insufficiency','chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis; fatty stools, low faecal elastase, normal D-xylose; fat-soluble vitamin deficiency'],
['Abetalipoproteinaemia','MTTP mutation (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein; autosomal recessive): apoB-48 and apoB-100 lipoproteins cannot be assembled, so no chylomicrons or VLDL; fat malabsorption in infancy, lipid-filled enterocytes, acanthocytes, very low lipids; vitamin E deficiency causes ataxia and retinitis pigmentosa']]},
s:[['bio','Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) travel with fat, so any steatorrhoea starves them: night blindness (A), osteomalacia (D), haemolysis and ataxia (E), and bleeding with a prolonged PT (K). B12 needs more than the ileum: acid and pepsin free it from food, pancreatic proteases free it from R-binder (haptocorrin) so intrinsic factor can take it, so pancreatic insufficiency can lower B12 too.']]},
{h:'Oesophagus and stomach',p:[
'**Achalasia**: loss of inhibitory (NO and VIP) neurons of the myenteric plexus, idiopathic or from Chagas disease; the lower sphincter will not relax and the body loses peristalsis, so dysphagia is to solids and liquids from the start; "bird\'s beak" on barium swallow, high resting pressure on manometry; a tumour at the cardia can mimic it (pseudoachalasia). **Scleroderma** does the opposite: smooth muscle atrophies, the sphincter is weak, and reflux follows. **Barrett oesophagus**: intestinal metaplasia (columnar cells with goblet cells) from reflux, the precursor of adenocarcinoma. **Mallory-Weiss tear** (mucosal, after retching, haematemesis) versus **Boerhaave rupture** (transmural, mediastinitis, subcutaneous emphysema). **Eosinophilic oesophagitis**: atopy, stacked rings, food impaction, poor response to PPIs. **Plummer-Vinson syndrome**: iron deficiency, oesophageal web, dysphagia, with a raised risk of squamous carcinoma. **Oesophageal varices** come with portal hypertension (see the Labyrinth).',
'**Zenker diverticulum** is a false (mucosa-only) pouch pushing out through Killian triangle above the upper sphincter in older people: halitosis, gurgling, and regurgitation of food eaten days before. **Hiatal hernia** is usually sliding (the gastro-oesophageal junction rises; reflux); a paraoesophageal hernia keeps the junction in place while the fundus rolls up beside the oesophagus and can strangulate. **Ménétrier disease**: giant hypertrophic gastric folds, protein-losing gastropathy with oedema, and too few parietal cells (low acid).',
'**Gastritis**: type A autoimmune (antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor; fundus and body; pernicious anaemia, achlorhydria, high gastrin, carcinoid risk) and type B **H. pylori** (antrum first; ulcers, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma). Acute erosive gastritis follows NSAIDs, alcohol and severe stress; **Curling ulcers** follow major burns (hypovolaemia, mucosal ischaemia) and **Cushing ulcers** follow brain injury (vagal drive raises acid). **Peptic ulcers**: duodenal ulcers are almost all H. pylori or NSAIDs, hurt less just after meals and are rarely malignant; gastric ulcers hurt more with meals and are biopsied, because a gastric cancer can look like an ulcer. Posterior duodenal ulcers bleed from the gastroduodenal artery; lesser-curvature gastric ulcers bleed from the left gastric artery. Perforation lets air under the diaphragm.'],
s:[['micro','H. pylori is a curved, flagellated, Gram-negative rod that makes urease; the ammonia it releases buffers a pocket of neutral pH under the mucus, which is also why the urea breath test and the rapid urease test find it. Antral infection kills D cells, gastrin rises and acid floods the duodenum (duodenal ulcer); body-predominant infection leads instead to atrophy, low acid and intestinal-type gastric cancer.'],['pharm','H. pylori: PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin (metronidazole if penicillin allergic); bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI, bismuth, metronidazole, tetracycline) where clarithromycin resistance is high. Confirm cure with a urea breath test or stool antigen, off PPIs for 2 weeks to avoid false negatives.'],['path','Pyloric stenosis in infants: hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle; projectile non-bilious vomiting at 2–6 weeks, an olive-shaped epigastric mass, hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis; commoner in first-born boys and after early erythromycin (a pharm link: the motilin agonist).']]},
{h:'Drugs of the forest floor',p:[
'Most gut drugs either change the acid, protect the lining, or change the speed of the tube. Choose by the mechanism and the side effects follow: anything that blocks dopamine in the gut also blocks it in the basal ganglia, anything that slows the tube constipates, and anything that coats the ulcer needs to be kept apart from drugs it would bind.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Action','Watch for'],rows:[
['Antacids','neutralise acid','aluminium constipates (and lowers phosphate); magnesium loosens stools; calcium carbonate can cause milk-alkali syndrome; all bind other drugs (tetracyclines, iron, quinolones)'],
['Bismuth subsalicylate','coats ulcers, antibacterial against H. pylori','black tongue and stools'],
['Sucralfate','aluminium sucrose sulfate that polymerises in acid and sticks to ulcer bases','needs acid, so not given with PPIs or antacids'],
['Misoprostol','PGE₁ analogue: ↑mucus and bicarbonate, ↓acid','prevents NSAID ulcers; diarrhoea; abortifacient (also used to induce labour and for postpartum haemorrhage), so avoid in pregnancy'],
['Metoclopramide','D₂ antagonist (and 5-HT₄ agonist): speeds gastric emptying','gastroparesis, nausea; parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, raised prolactin; never in bowel obstruction'],
['Ondansetron','5-HT₃ antagonist in the gut and brainstem','chemotherapy and postoperative nausea; constipation, headache, long QT'],
['Aprepitant','NK₁ (substance P) antagonist','chemotherapy nausea, added to ondansetron'],
['Loperamide','peripheral μ-opioid agonist that barely enters the brain','diarrhoea; avoid in bloody (invasive) diarrhoea; cardiac toxicity in overdose'],
['Osmotic laxatives','polyethylene glycol, lactulose, magnesium salts draw water into the lumen','bloating; lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy'],
['Stimulant laxatives','senna, bisacodyl stimulate the myenteric plexus','cramps; senna can stain the colon brown (melanosis coli)'],
['Bulk-forming laxatives','psyllium, bran hold water in the stool','take with fluid; bloating'],
['Orlistat','inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase','weight loss; oily stools, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency']]},
key:'Sucralfate needs acid; misoprostol is a prostaglandin; metoclopramide is a dopamine blocker. Each side effect follows from that one fact.'},
{h:'Inflammatory bowel disease',p:[
'Both diseases are chronic, relapsing immune attacks on the bowel in genetically primed people, triggered by the gut flora, but they differ in where they strike and how deep they cut. **Crohn disease** is patchy, transmural and granulomatous and can reach any segment from mouth to anus. Because it runs through the whole wall it tunnels (fistulas to bowel, bladder, skin and vagina), scars (strictures and obstruction) and glues loops together (abscesses, the "creeping fat" of mesentery wrapping the bowel). NOD2 variants, which weaken sensing of bacterial wall peptides, raise the risk. Symptoms: crampy right lower quadrant pain, often non-bloody diarrhoea, weight loss, perianal disease and malabsorption when the ileum is involved.',
'**Ulcerative colitis** is a continuous mucosal disease that always begins in the rectum and spreads proximally without gaps. Because it stays superficial it bleeds and ulcerates rather than fistulating: bloody diarrhoea with urgency and tenesmus. Its gravest acute complication is **toxic megacolon**, and its long-term threat is colorectal cancer, which rises with the extent and duration of colitis (both diseases need surveillance colonoscopy when the colon has been involved for about 8 years).'],
table:{cols:['Feature','Crohn disease','Ulcerative colitis'],rows:[
['Location','anywhere mouth to anus, terminal ileum most, skip lesions, rectum often spared','rectum always, extending continuously up the colon'],
['Depth','transmural: fistulas, strictures, abscesses','mucosa and submucosa only'],
['Gross','cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, "string sign" of a narrowed ileum','pseudopolyps, loss of haustra ("lead pipe")'],
['Microscopy','non-caseating granulomas, lymphoid aggregates (Th1 and Th17 driven)','crypt abscesses, distorted crypts, ulcers (Th2-like cytokines)'],
['Complications','malabsorption, B12 deficiency, calcium oxalate kidney stones, gallstones, perianal disease','toxic megacolon, primary sclerosing cholangitis (p-ANCA), colorectal cancer'],
['Serology','ASCA (anti-Saccharomyces) more often','p-ANCA more often'],
['Smoking','worsens','seems protective']]},
s:[['pharm','Mesalazine (5-ASA) for mild to moderate UC; sulfasalazine is 5-ASA joined to sulfapyridine, which carries the sulfa side effects (rash, reversible oligospermia, low folate). Steroids for flares only. Maintenance: azathioprine (check TPMT first), methotrexate; anti-TNF (infliximab, adalimumab: screen for latent TB), vedolizumab (α4β7 integrin, gut-selective), ustekinumab (IL-12/23), JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib). Colectomy cures UC, but surgery never cures Crohn.'],['path','Both: erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, uveitis and episcleritis, ankylosing spondylitis and sacroiliitis, migratory arthritis. Irritable bowel syndrome has pain linked to defecation with a change in stool form but no inflammation and normal tests; diagnosis by Rome criteria. Microscopic colitis (older women, NSAIDs or PPIs) gives watery diarrhoea with a normal-looking colon and abnormal biopsies.']]},
{h:'Bowel emergencies',p:[
'**Appendicitis**: obstruction of the lumen (faecolith, lymphoid hyperplasia in children); periumbilical pain (visceral, T10) that moves to McBurney point when the parietal peritoneum is inflamed. **Diverticulosis**: false diverticula where the vasa recta pierce the sigmoid wall, from high pressure and low fibre; diverticular bleeding is painless, and **diverticulitis** gives left lower quadrant pain and fever, sometimes with abscess or a colovesical fistula (pneumaturia). **Hirschsprung disease**: neural crest cells fail to migrate to the distal colon, so the rectum has no ganglion cells (RET mutations, Down syndrome): delayed meconium beyond 48 hours, an empty rectum and a blast of stool on rectal examination.',
'**Intussusception**: children 6–36 months, colicky pain with drawn-up legs, red currant-jelly stool and a sausage-shaped mass; the lead point is often enlarged Peyer patches after a viral infection (or Meckel diverticulum, or a tumour in adults); air enema reduces it. **Volvulus**: sigmoid in older adults (coffee-bean sign), caecal, and midgut volvulus from malrotation in infants, with bilious vomiting as an emergency. **Meckel diverticulum** is a true diverticulum, a remnant of the vitelline duct: about 2% of people, about 60 cm (2 feet) from the ileocaecal valve, usually symptomatic before age 2, and can contain ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue that ulcerates and bleeds painlessly (technetium-99m pertechnetate scan). **Mesenteric ischaemia**: pain out of proportion to the examination, often atrial fibrillation (embolus) or atherosclerosis; watershed zones (splenic flexure, rectosigmoid) suffer in low-flow states. **Necrotising enterocolitis**: premature infants, distension and bloody stool, gas in the bowel wall (pneumatosis intestinalis).'],
table:{cols:['Hernia','Route','Who and why'],rows:[
['Indirect inguinal','through the deep inguinal ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, into the scrotum','patent processus vaginalis; infants and young men; the commonest hernia'],
['Direct inguinal','through Hesselbach triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels; does not reach the scrotum','weak transversalis fascia in older men'],
['Femoral','below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein','women; the most likely to strangulate'],
['Congenital diaphragmatic','posterolateral (Bochdalek) defect, usually left','bowel in the chest, pulmonary hypoplasia in the newborn']]},
s:[['path','Angiodysplasia (dilated submucosal vessels, right colon, older people) bleeds painlessly and is associated with aortic stenosis (Heyde syndrome: shear stress destroys von Willebrand multimers). Ischaemic colitis in older vascular patients gives pain then bloody diarrhoea at the watershed zones.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A woman has right upper quadrant pain 30 minutes after a fatty meal. Which hormone, released by duodenal I cells, made her gallbladder contract?',o:['Secretin','Gastrin','Motilin','CCK'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'gi-hormones',x:'Fat and protein in the duodenum make I cells release cholecystokinin, which contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi and slows gastric emptying, so a fatty meal provokes biliary colic. Secretin from S cells answers acid with bicarbonate; it does not drive gallbladder contraction.'},
{q:'A woman has iron-deficiency anaemia, an itchy blistering rash on her elbows and bloating after bread. Her total IgA is normal. Best first test?',o:['ANA','Anti-tTG IgA','p-ANCA','Faecal elastase'],a:1,d:'path',c:'malabsorption',x:'This is coeliac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis: gliadin, deamidated by tissue transglutaminase and presented on HLA-DQ2/DQ8, drives T-cell injury, so IgA anti-tTG is the screen once total IgA is known to be normal. Duodenal biopsy then shows villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia. Faecal elastase tests the pancreas, which does not cause the rash.'},
{q:'A 24-year-old smoker has weight loss, right lower quadrant pain and a perianal fistula; biopsy shows transmural inflammation with non-caseating granulomas. Disease, and the effect of smoking?',o:['Crohn; smoking worsens','UC; smoking protects','UC; smoking worsens','Crohn; smoking protects'],a:0,d:'path',c:'ibd',x:'Transmural, patchy, granulomatous inflammation with fistulas is Crohn disease, and smoking worsens it and raises relapse after surgery. Ulcerative colitis is mucosal and continuous from the rectum with crypt abscesses; it is the disease smoking seems to protect against, which is why that option tempts.'},
{q:'A 4-week-old first-born boy has projectile non-bilious vomiting after every feed and a small firm epigastric mass. Expected blood gas?',o:['High anion gap metabolic acidosis','Respiratory alkalosis with low CO₂','Hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis','Hyperchloraemic normal-gap acidosis'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'acid-base',x:'The hypertrophied pylorus blocks the outlet, so the infant vomits HCl; volume contraction then drives aldosterone, which wastes K⁺ and H⁺ in the urine (paradoxical aciduria). The result is hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, corrected with saline and potassium before pyloromyotomy. A normal-gap acidosis would follow diarrhoea, not vomiting.'},
{q:'A man has had 100 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. Which deficiency is most expected over the next few years?',o:['Iron','Pyridoxine (B6)','Folate','Vitamin B12'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'b12-folate',x:'Intrinsic factor–B12 complexes are taken up only by receptors in the terminal ileum, so ileal resection causes B12 deficiency once liver stores, which last years, run out; losing bile-salt recycling adds fat malabsorption. Iron is absorbed in the duodenum and folate in the jejunum, so both are spared.'},
{q:'A woman with rheumatoid arthritis needs long-term NSAIDs and has had a gastric ulcer. Which protective drug replaces the prostaglandin the NSAID removes, and must be avoided in pregnancy?',o:['Sucralfate','Misoprostol','Bismuth','Ondansetron'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'peptic-ulcer',x:'NSAIDs block COX and remove the prostaglandins that keep mucus, bicarbonate and mucosal blood flow up; misoprostol, a PGE₁ analogue, puts them back, but it contracts the uterus (an abortifacient) and causes diarrhoea. Sucralfate also protects ulcer beds, but by forming an acid-dependent coat, not by replacing prostaglandin.'},
{q:'A 78-year-old man has bad breath, gurgling in his neck and regurgitates food he ate two days earlier. Barium swallow shows a pouch behind the upper oesophagus. Diagnosis?',o:['Zenker diverticulum','Achalasia of the cardia','Plummer-Vinson web','Barrett oesophagus'],a:0,d:'path',x:'Zenker diverticulum is a false pouch of mucosa that bulges through Killian triangle above the upper oesophageal sphincter; trapped food rots (halitosis) and comes back undigested. Achalasia also gives regurgitation, but of a dilated oesophagus above a tight lower sphincter, with a bird\'s beak rather than a pouch.'}
]};

BOOK['c11-liver']={depth:11,title:'The Hepatic Labyrinth',
epi:'A labyrinth that filters everything the gut sends up. Its walls are scarring, room by room.',
c:['cirrhosis','viral-hepatitis','bilirubin','liver-metabolic','ethanol','ammonia'],
S:[
{h:'The lobule',p:[
'Blood enters at the portal triads (hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct), runs through the sinusoids and leaves by the central vein; bile runs the other way, from canaliculi toward the triad. Oxygen and nutrients fall along the way, and so the cells along each sinusoid specialise. **Zone 1 (periportal)** gets the richest blood and does most gluconeogenesis, β-oxidation and urea synthesis; it is hit first by blood-borne viruses and by ingested toxins (cocaine). **Zone 2 (midzonal)** is the target of yellow fever (Councilman bodies). **Zone 3 (centrilobular)** gets the least oxygen and carries the most CYP450: it dies first in ischaemia ("shock liver"), in paracetamol poisoning and with alcohol, and congests in right heart failure ("nutmeg liver").',
'The other residents: **Kupffer cells** are macrophages lining the sinusoids; **stellate (Ito) cells** in the space of Disse store vitamin A and, when activated by injury, turn into myofibroblasts that lay down the collagen of fibrosis. The liver makes albumin and most clotting factors (II, VII, IX and X need vitamin K; factor VII has the shortest half-life, so the PT is the first test to rise), turns ammonia into urea, and makes bile acids from cholesterol.'],
table:{cols:['Test','Meaning'],rows:[['ALT, AST','hepatocyte injury; AST:ALT above 2 suggests alcohol (alcohol depletes B6, which ALT needs more); thousands in viral, ischaemic or toxic hepatitis'],['ALP, GGT','cholestasis (ALP also rises from bone and placenta); a raised GGT confirms the liver as the source'],['Albumin, PT/INR','synthetic function; the INR changes within a day, albumin over weeks'],['Bilirubin','conjugated or unconjugated (see the next room)'],['Ammonia','hepatic encephalopathy (a poor guide to its severity)']]},
s:[['path','Budd-Chiari syndrome: thrombosis of the hepatic veins (polycythaemia vera and other myeloproliferative disorders, pregnancy, oral contraceptives, malignancy) gives painful hepatomegaly, ascites and centrilobular congestion. Reye syndrome: aspirin given to a child with a viral illness (influenza, varicella) causes microvesicular fatty liver and encephalopathy, because mitochondrial β-oxidation fails; avoid aspirin under 16 except for Kawasaki disease.']]},
{h:'Bilirubin and jaundice',fig:'bili',p:[
'Haem → biliverdin (haem oxygenase) → **unconjugated bilirubin**, which is fat-soluble, travels bound to albumin and never appears in urine → taken up by the liver and **conjugated** with glucuronic acid by UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1) → pumped into bile (MRP2) → gut bacteria make urobilinogen (some reabsorbed and passed in urine) and stercobilin (brown stool).',
'**Unconjugated** excess: haemolysis, newborn jaundice, **Gilbert syndrome** (mildly reduced UGT1A1 from a promoter variant; harmless yellowing during fasting, illness or exercise), **Crigler-Najjar** (autosomal recessive; type I has no UGT activity and causes kernicterus, type II has some and responds to phenobarbital, which induces UGT). **Conjugated** excess: obstruction (gallstones, pancreatic head cancer, biliary atresia; pale stools, dark urine, itch from bile salts, high ALP), hepatitis, **Dubin-Johnson syndrome** (MRP2 defect; black liver from pigment, harmless) and **Rotor syndrome** (milder, no pigment).'],
table:{cols:['Pattern','Bilirubin','Urine','Stool','Examples'],rows:[
['Pre-hepatic','unconjugated','no bilirubin; urobilinogen ↑','normal or dark','haemolysis, Gilbert, Crigler-Najjar'],
['Hepatocellular','mixed','bilirubin present; urobilinogen variable','normal or pale','viral, alcoholic or drug hepatitis, cirrhosis'],
['Obstructive','conjugated','dark, bilirubin present; urobilinogen ↓','pale (acholic)','stones, pancreatic head cancer, biliary atresia, PBC, PSC']]},
s:[['phys','Neonatal jaundice: UGT is immature and fetal red cells turn over fast. Phototherapy turns bilirubin into water-soluble isomers that need no conjugation. Kernicterus: unconjugated bilirubin crosses the immature blood-brain barrier into the basal ganglia. Jaundice in the first 24 hours of life is never physiological (think haemolysis).']]},
{h:'Hepatitis',p:[
'Viral hepatitis: **A and E** spread faecal-orally and are usually acute only (E can become chronic in transplant recipients and other immunosuppressed people, and is dangerous in pregnancy, with fulminant hepatitis in the third trimester). **B, C and D** spread by blood, sex and from mother to child. **Hepatitis B**: adult infection usually clears; neonatal infection usually becomes chronic. Chronic B raises the risk of cirrhosis and **hepatocellular carcinoma** (even without cirrhosis) and is linked to polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy. **Hepatitis C**: usually chronic; cirrhosis, HCC, mixed cryoglobulinaemia, membranoproliferative GN, porphyria cutanea tarda, lichen planus; cured in most by direct-acting antivirals. **Hepatitis D** needs HBsAg to build its coat: caught with B (coinfection) it usually clears with B, but landing on chronic B (superinfection) is severe.',
'**Alcohol-related liver disease**: fatty liver (reversible within weeks of abstinence) → alcoholic hepatitis (ballooned hepatocytes, Mallory-Denk bodies of damaged keratin, neutrophils, AST:ALT above 2) → cirrhosis. **Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease** (MASLD, formerly NAFLD) comes with obesity, type 2 diabetes and insulin resistance; ALT is usually above AST; its inflammatory form (MASH) can scar to cirrhosis. **Autoimmune hepatitis**: young and middle-aged women, high IgG; type 1 anti-smooth muscle and ANA, type 2 anti-LKM1 (children); responds to steroids. Drug injury: paracetamol (zone 3 necrosis), isoniazid, valproate (microvesicular steatosis), amiodarone, methotrexate (fibrosis), statins, co-amoxiclav and anabolic steroids (cholestasis).'],
table:{cols:['Hepatitis B marker','Meaning'],rows:[
['HBsAg','infection now (acute or chronic); still present after 6 months = chronic'],
['Anti-HBs','immunity, from vaccine or recovery'],
['HBeAg','active replication, high infectivity'],
['Anti-HBe','replication slowing, lower infectivity'],
['IgM anti-HBc','recent infection; the only marker in the window period, when HBsAg has gone and anti-HBs has not yet appeared'],
['IgG anti-HBc','past or chronic infection; never made after vaccination alone'],
['HBV DNA','viral load; guides treatment']]},
key:'Vaccinated: anti-HBs alone. Recovered: anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc. Window period: IgM anti-HBc alone. Chronic: HBsAg beyond 6 months.',
s:[['micro','Hepatitis A is a picornavirus (RNA, no envelope, survives acid and bile); B is a hepadnavirus, a partly double-stranded DNA virus that copies itself through an RNA intermediate with its own reverse transcriptase; C is a flavivirus (RNA) whose hypervariable envelope defeats antibody, so there is no vaccine; D is a defective RNA virus; E is a hepevirus, carried by undercooked pork and contaminated water.'],['pharm','Hepatitis B: tenofovir or entecavir (nucleos(t)ide analogues that block its reverse transcriptase), or peginterferon. Newborns of HBsAg-positive mothers get vaccine plus hepatitis B immunoglobulin within 12 hours. Hepatitis C: combinations of NS5A inhibitors (-asvir: ledipasvir, velpatasvir), NS5B polymerase inhibitors (sofosbuvir, -buvir) and NS3/4A protease inhibitors (-previr) for 8–12 weeks.']]},
{h:'Cirrhosis and its consequences',p:[
'Cirrhosis is diffuse fibrosis that cuts the liver into regenerative nodules, so blood can no longer flow freely from portal vein to central vein and the cells that remain have lost their architecture. **Portal hypertension** opens the portosystemic shunts: left gastric to oesophageal veins (**varices**), paraumbilical to abdominal wall veins (**caput medusae**), superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal veins (anorectal varices). It also causes **ascites** (high serum-ascites albumin gradient, 11 g/L (1.1 g/dL) or more) and **splenomegaly** with low platelets. A transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) lowers the pressure but worsens encephalopathy.',
'**Liver failure**: jaundice, coagulopathy, low albumin, **hepatic encephalopathy** (ammonia and other gut toxins bypass the liver; asterixis, confusion; triggered by GI bleeding, infection, constipation, sedatives, low K⁺ and diuretics), hyperoestrogenism (spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, testicular atrophy). **Hepatorenal syndrome**: intense renal vasoconstriction in a structurally normal kidney (low urine sodium; albumin and terlipressin). **Hepatopulmonary syndrome**: intrapulmonary shunts, hypoxaemia worse when upright. **Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis**: ascitic neutrophils above 250/µL, usually E. coli or Klebsiella; third-generation cephalosporin, then prophylaxis.'],
table:{cols:['Metabolic liver disease','Clues'],rows:[
['Hereditary haemochromatosis','HFE C282Y (autosomal recessive, chromosome 6); low hepcidin, so iron absorption is unchecked; high ferritin and transferrin saturation, low TIBC; cirrhosis, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy of the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints, hypogonadism, HCC; phlebotomy (chelation with deferoxamine or deferasirox in transfusion overload)'],
['Wilson disease','ATP7B (autosomal recessive, chromosome 13): copper is not loaded onto caeruloplasmin or excreted in bile; low caeruloplasmin, high urinary copper, Kayser-Fleischer rings, basal ganglia (parkinsonism, dystonia, psychiatric change), haemolysis; penicillamine, trientine, zinc'],
['α₁-antitrypsin deficiency','PiZZ: misfolded protein trapped in the hepatocyte ER (PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules) injures the liver; lack of it in the blood allows panacinar emphysema; neonatal hepatitis'],
['Primary biliary cholangitis','middle-aged women; autoimmune destruction of small intrahepatic ducts; anti-mitochondrial antibodies; itch, fatigue, high ALP, xanthelasma; Sjögren and other autoimmunity; ursodeoxycholic acid'],
['Primary sclerosing cholangitis','men with UC; p-ANCA; inflammation and onion-skin fibrosis of large intra- and extrahepatic ducts, "beaded" on MRCP; cholangiocarcinoma and colorectal cancer risk; transplant']]},
s:[['pharm','Varices: a non-selective β-blocker (propranolol, carvedilol) lowers portal inflow and prevents bleeding, with band ligation. Acute variceal bleed: octreotide or terlipressin (splanchnic vasoconstriction), antibiotics, endoscopic banding. Encephalopathy: lactulose (acidifies the colon, trapping NH₄⁺) and rifaximin (kills ammonia-making bacteria). Ascites: salt restriction, spironolactone first plus furosemide, large-volume paracentesis with albumin.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'After a cardiac arrest with 20 minutes of low blood pressure, a man\'s ALT rises to 4,000 U/L within a day. Which part of the lobule died first?',o:['Zone 3 (centrilobular)','Zone 1 (periportal)','Zone 2 (midzonal)','Bile ductules near the triad'],a:0,d:'path',c:'cirrhosis',x:'Centrilobular hepatocytes sit at the end of the sinusoid where oxygen is lowest, so hypotension kills them first ("shock liver"); transaminases rise into the thousands within a day and then fall quickly. Zone 1 is hit first by blood-borne viruses and ingested toxins, and zone 2 by yellow fever.'},
{q:'A healthy 22-year-old turns mildly yellow during a three-day fast. Bilirubin is unconjugated; blood count, reticulocytes and liver enzymes are normal. Diagnosis?',o:['Crigler-Najjar type I','Dubin-Johnson syndrome','Gilbert syndrome','Haemolytic anaemia'],a:2,d:'bio',c:'bilirubin',x:'Gilbert syndrome is mildly reduced UGT1A1 activity (a promoter variant) that shows only under stress such as fasting, illness or exertion, with unconjugated bilirubin and otherwise normal tests; it is harmless. Crigler-Najjar type I has near-absent UGT and causes kernicterus in infancy, and Dubin-Johnson raises conjugated bilirubin.'},
{q:'A 52-year-old man has diabetes, bronze skin, arthritis of the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints and a transferrin saturation of 80%. Treatment?',o:['Penicillamine','Prednisolone','Ursodeoxycholic acid','Regular phlebotomy'],a:3,d:'path',c:'liver-metabolic',x:'Hereditary haemochromatosis (HFE C282Y) lowers hepcidin, so the gut absorbs iron without limit and it deposits in liver, pancreas, skin, joints, heart and gonads. Removing iron by regular phlebotomy is the treatment, with chelation for those who cannot be bled. Penicillamine chelates copper in Wilson disease.'},
{q:'A man with cirrhosis becomes confused with a flapping tremor two days after a variceal bleed. First-line drug?',o:['Furosemide','Lactulose','Lorazepam','Propranolol'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'ammonia',x:'Blood in the gut is a protein load that bacteria turn into ammonia, which the failing liver and its shunts let through to the brain. Lactulose is fermented to acids that trap ammonia as ammonium and speeds transit; rifaximin is added to prevent relapse. Propranolol prevents rebleeding but does not treat the confusion, and benzodiazepines worsen it.'},
{q:'An itchy 50-year-old woman has fatigue, a raised ALP, normal bile ducts on ultrasound and anti-mitochondrial antibodies. Diagnosis?',o:['Autoimmune hepatitis','Primary sclerosing cholangitis','Choledocholithiasis','Primary biliary cholangitis'],a:3,d:'path',c:'cirrhosis',x:'Primary biliary cholangitis is autoimmune destruction of the small intrahepatic bile ducts in middle-aged women, with anti-mitochondrial antibodies, itch and a cholestatic (high ALP) picture while the large ducts look normal. PSC typically affects men with ulcerative colitis, with p-ANCA and beaded large ducts.'},
{q:'A newly qualified nurse has anti-HBs positive, HBsAg negative and anti-HBc negative. Interpretation?',o:['Immune from vaccination','Recovered from past infection','Window period of acute infection','Chronic infection, low infectivity'],a:0,d:'micro',c:'viral-hepatitis',x:'The vaccine contains only HBsAg, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs but never anti-HBc. Recovery from real infection leaves anti-HBs together with IgG anti-HBc, and the window period shows IgM anti-HBc with neither HBsAg nor anti-HBs.'},
{q:'A 19-year-old has a tremor, new low mood, a haemolytic anaemia and golden-brown rings at the edge of each cornea. Which result is expected?',o:['High serum caeruloplasmin','Low serum caeruloplasmin','High transferrin saturation','Low α₁-antitrypsin'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'liver-metabolic',x:'Wilson disease (ATP7B, autosomal recessive) stops the liver loading copper onto caeruloplasmin and excreting it in bile, so caeruloplasmin is low while free copper floods the liver, basal ganglia, cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings) and red cells. A high transferrin saturation is the screen for iron overload in haemochromatosis.'}
]};

BOOK['c11-pancreas']={depth:11,title:'The Ferment Glands',
epi:'A gland full of enzymes that can digest flesh. When it bursts, it digests itself.',
c:['pancreatitis','gallstones','cystic-fibrosis','ethanol','calcium-pth','men'],
S:[
{h:'The gland and its juice',p:[
'The pancreas grows from two buds of the foregut. The **ventral bud** forms the uncinate process, part of the head and the main duct; the **dorsal bud** forms the rest. When the ventral bud swings around the duodenum it can encircle it (**annular pancreas**: duodenal obstruction, Down syndrome). If the two ducts fail to fuse (**pancreas divisum**, the commonest variant) most juice drains through the small minor papilla, usually harmlessly, sometimes with recurrent pancreatitis.',
'Old anatomists called enzymes ferments, and this gland is full of them. **Acinar cells** make the enzymes, stored as inactive zymogens, under CCK and vagal ACh. **Ductal cells** add a large volume of **alkaline** juice rich in bicarbonate under secretin, moving Cl⁻ out through CFTR and swapping it back for HCO₃⁻; in cystic fibrosis the juice is thick and scant, the ducts plug, and the gland destroys itself from childhood. The juice neutralises gastric acid so that the enzymes, which work at near-neutral pH, can act.'],
s:[['phys','Pancreatic enzymes are secreted as zymogens; trypsinogen is cut to trypsin by enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the duodenal brush border, and trypsin then activates the rest and more trypsinogen. The gland protects itself with trypsin inhibitor (SPINK1) and with trypsin that can destroy itself; gain of function in PRSS1 (cationic trypsinogen) or loss of SPINK1 causes hereditary pancreatitis.']]},
{h:'Acute pancreatitis',p:[
'Enzymes activated inside the gland digest it, its fat and its vessels. Causes: **gallstones** (blocking the ampulla) and **alcohol** cause most; then hypertriglyceridaemia (above about 11 mmol/L or 1,000 mg/dL), hypercalcaemia, ERCP, drugs (azathioprine, valproate, GLP-1 agonists, thiazides, didanosine, sulfonamides), mumps and other viruses, scorpion venom, trauma and hypothermia.',
'Diagnosis needs two of three: severe epigastric pain radiating to the back (eased by leaning forward), **lipase** (more specific) or amylase above 3 times normal, and typical imaging. Complications: fat necrosis with **hypocalcaemia** (calcium soaps), ARDS, DIC and shock, haemorrhage (Grey Turner flank and Cullen periumbilical bruising), **pseudocyst** (a collection walled by granulation and fibrous tissue, not epithelium, after about 4 weeks), and necrosis that can become infected. Treat with early aggressive fluids, analgesia and early oral feeding; urgent ERCP only if gallstone cholangitis; cholecystectomy in the same admission for gallstone disease.'],
s:[['path','Enzymatic fat necrosis: lipase splits triglyceride in peripancreatic fat; the free fatty acids bind calcium and form chalky white deposits that look blue-purple (basophilic) on H&E (saponification). Haemorrhagic pancreatitis follows when elastase digests vessel walls.']]},
{h:'Chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer',p:[
'**Chronic pancreatitis**: repeated inflammation leaves fibrosis, duct strictures and calcification; alcohol in adults, cystic fibrosis in children, also PRSS1, SPINK1 and autoimmune (IgG4) disease. Calcifications on CT, a "chain of lakes" duct, chronic pain, steatorrhoea once more than about 90% of the exocrine gland is lost, and diabetes as the islets are destroyed. Replace enzymes with meals.',
'**Pancreatic adenocarcinoma** arises from ductal cells through precursors (PanIN, and cystic IPMNs), with KRAS activation early and loss of CDKN2A (p16), TP53 and SMAD4 later; the tumour provokes a dense desmoplastic stroma. Risk: smoking (strongest), chronic pancreatitis, obesity, diabetes, age, BRCA2 and Lynch syndrome. **Head** tumours block the bile duct and cause **painless obstructive jaundice** with a palpable, non-tender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign); **body and tail** tumours present late with back pain and weight loss. New-onset diabetes in an older thin adult is a warning. Migratory thrombophlebitis (**Trousseau syndrome**, from mucins and tissue factor) can appear. CA 19-9 follows the disease; prognosis is poor (a Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy if resectable).'],
table:{cols:['Islet tumour','Clues'],rows:[['Insulinoma','commonest islet tumour; fasting hypoglycaemia relieved by glucose (Whipple triad), high insulin and C-peptide, raised proinsulin; MEN1'],['Gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison)','multiple, distal duodenal and jejunal ulcers, diarrhoea (acid inactivates lipase); gastrin rises after IV secretin; usually in the duodenum or pancreatic head; MEN1 in about a quarter'],['Glucagonoma','necrolytic migratory erythema, diabetes, weight loss, venous thrombosis'],['Somatostatinoma','gallstones, diabetes, steatorrhoea, low acid'],['VIPoma','watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, achlorhydria']]},
s:[['pharm','Pancreatic enzyme replacement (pancrelipase) with every meal and snack, with a PPI if acid destroys it. Gastrinoma: high-dose PPI. Somatostatin analogues (octreotide) control symptoms of most hormone-secreting islet tumours, apart from many insulinomas, which lack the receptor.']]},
{h:'Gallbladder and bile ducts',p:[
'Bile acids are made from cholesterol (7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme), conjugated with glycine or taurine, and recycled several times a day. Cholesterol stays dissolved only when bile acids and lecithin hold it in micelles: too much cholesterol, too few bile acids or a sluggish gallbladder lets it crystallise.',
'**Biliary colic**: a stone briefly blocks the cystic duct after a fatty meal (CCK); pain eases within hours. **Acute cholecystitis**: the stone stays stuck, the wall inflames, pain persists with fever and **Murphy sign**; ultrasound shows a thick wall and fluid; acalculous cholecystitis occurs in the critically ill. **Choledocholithiasis**: a stone in the common bile duct, jaundice and high ALP (and pancreatitis if it blocks the ampulla). **Ascending cholangitis**: infection behind the blockage, Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain) plus shock and confusion (Reynolds pentad); urgent drainage by ERCP. **Gallstone ileus**: a stone erodes through a fistula into the bowel and lodges at the ileocaecal valve; air in the biliary tree (pneumobilia). **Porcelain gallbladder** (calcified wall) and gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma.',
'In infants, **biliary atresia** is a progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic ducts: conjugated jaundice beyond 2 weeks of age, pale stools, dark urine and a firm liver; a Kasai portoenterostomy works best before about 8 weeks. A **choledochal cyst** (dilated bile duct) gives pain, jaundice and a mass, and carries cancer risk.'],
table:{cols:['Stone','Made of','Risk','X-ray'],rows:[
['Cholesterol (most)','cholesterol crystals, yellow','female sex, pregnancy and oestrogen, obesity, rapid weight loss, age over 40, fibrates (block 7α-hydroxylase), ileal Crohn or resection, some Indigenous American peoples','usually lucent'],
['Black pigment','calcium bilirubinate','chronic haemolysis (sickle cell, spherocytosis), cirrhosis, ileal disease','often opaque'],
['Brown pigment','calcium salts with bacterial debris','infection of the ducts (E. coli, Clonorchis and other liver flukes, common in East Asia)','lucent']]},
s:[['pharm','Ursodeoxycholic acid slowly dissolves small cholesterol stones and is first-line in primary biliary cholangitis; cholecystectomy is the definitive treatment for symptomatic stones. Ceftriaxone can precipitate as biliary sludge; octreotide and fibrates promote stones.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'On day 3 of severe alcohol-related pancreatitis, a man has perioral tingling and a corrected calcium of 1.8 mmol/L. Mechanism?',o:['Calcitonin released from the thyroid','Calcium soaps in fat necrosis','PTH suppressed by alcohol excess','Renal calcium wasting by diuretics'],a:1,d:'path',c:'pancreatitis',x:'Lipase released into peripancreatic fat frees fatty acids that bind calcium as chalky soaps (saponification), pulling calcium out of the blood; a low calcium is a marker of severe disease. Low magnesium in heavy drinkers can add to it by blunting PTH, but the main cause here is fat necrosis.'},
{q:'A 70-year-old smoker has painless jaundice, weight loss, new-onset diabetes and a palpable, non-tender gallbladder. Most likely?',o:['Stone in the common bile duct','Chronic pancreatitis from alcohol misuse','Acute viral hepatitis','Pancreatic head adenocarcinoma'],a:3,d:'path',x:'A slowly growing tumour in the pancreatic head blocks the common bile duct gradually, so the healthy gallbladder distends and can be felt (Courvoisier sign) and jaundice is painless; new diabetes and weight loss add weight. A duct stone usually causes pain, and its gallbladder is scarred by old inflammation and does not distend.'},
{q:'Fever, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain, followed by hypotension and confusion. Key step after fluids and antibiotics?',o:['Ursodeoxycholic acid tablets','Elective cholecystectomy next month','Urgent biliary drainage by ERCP','Repeat ultrasound in one week'],a:2,d:'micro',c:'gallstones',x:'Charcot triad plus shock and confusion is the Reynolds pentad of ascending cholangitis: infected bile under pressure behind a stone, usually gut bacteria such as E. coli, Klebsiella and enterococci. Antibiotics alone fail until the duct is drained, so urgent ERCP decompresses it; cholecystectomy comes later to prevent another attack.'},
{q:'Which brush-border enzyme of the duodenum starts the activation cascade of the pancreatic zymogens?',o:['Trypsin','Carboxypeptidase A','Pepsin','Enterokinase'],a:3,d:'bio',c:'pancreatitis',x:'Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the duodenal brush border cuts trypsinogen to trypsin, and trypsin then activates the other zymogens and more trypsinogen. Trypsin is the amplifier, not the starter; when trypsin is activated inside the gland (PRSS1 gain, SPINK1 loss) the pancreas digests itself.'},
{q:'A man has jejunal ulcers, diarrhoea and a fasting gastrin that rises further after IV secretin. Associated inherited syndrome?',o:['MEN1','MEN2A','MEN2B','Von Hippel-Lindau'],a:0,d:'path',c:'men',x:'The secretin paradox marks a gastrinoma: secretin suppresses normal G cells but makes gastrinoma cells release more gastrin. About a quarter of gastrinomas occur in MEN1 (menin), with parathyroid and pituitary tumours. MEN2 syndromes bring medullary thyroid cancer and phaeochromocytoma instead.'},
{q:'A 6-week-old has jaundice, pale stools and dark urine; most of the bilirubin is conjugated. Most likely cause needing urgent surgery?',o:['Breast-milk jaundice','Biliary atresia','Gilbert syndrome','Crigler-Najjar type II'],a:1,d:'path',c:'bilirubin',x:'Biliary atresia is a progressive fibrosing obliteration of the extrahepatic ducts: conjugated jaundice beyond 2 weeks with acholic stools and dark urine. A Kasai portoenterostomy works best before about 8 weeks, and it is the commonest reason children receive liver transplants. Breast-milk jaundice raises unconjugated bilirubin with normal stool colour.'}
]};

BOOK['c11-gicancer']={depth:11,title:'The Rot Below',
epi:'Beneath the forest floor, polyps grow slowly in the dark. Given time, some of them turn.',
c:['colorectal-cancer','p53-rb','oncogenes','dna-repair','ibd','viral-hepatitis'],
S:[
{h:'Polyps to carcinoma',p:[
'Most colorectal cancers grow from **adenomas** over about ten years through the **adenoma-carcinoma sequence**, the slow turn the epigraph describes. Loss of **APC** comes first: APC normally helps destroy β-catenin, so without it Wnt signalling keeps crypt cells dividing and a small adenoma forms. **KRAS** activation then lets the adenoma grow, and loss of **TP53** turns it into carcinoma, all with chromosomal instability (about 85% of cancers). A second route is **microsatellite instability**: loss of mismatch repair (Lynch syndrome, or sporadic MLH1 promoter methylation) leaves frameshifts in short repeats; these tumours are right-sided, mucinous and full of lymphocytes. A third, the **serrated pathway** (BRAF mutation, CpG island methylation), runs through sessile serrated lesions, mostly on the right, which are flat and easy to miss.',
'Adenomas at higher risk are large (above 1 cm), villous rather than tubular, and highly dysplastic. Small hyperplastic polyps of the rectosigmoid are usually harmless. **Hamartomatous polyps** grow in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome and juvenile polyposis; **inflammatory pseudopolyps** in ulcerative colitis are islands of surviving mucosa, not tumours.'],
table:{cols:['Syndrome','Gene','Features'],rows:[['Familial adenomatous polyposis','APC (autosomal dominant)','hundreds to thousands of adenomas by the teens, cancer by 40 without surgery; prophylactic colectomy; Gardner variant (osteomas, desmoid and soft tissue tumours, extra teeth, congenital retinal pigment hypertrophy), Turcot variant (medulloblastoma)'],['Lynch syndrome (hereditary non-polyposis)','MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 (autosomal dominant)','few polyps that progress fast; right-sided colon, endometrial (the commonest cancer in women carriers), ovarian, stomach, small bowel, urinary tract'],['MUTYH-associated polyposis','MUTYH (autosomal recessive)','tens to hundreds of adenomas; base excision repair of oxidised guanine fails'],['Peutz-Jeghers syndrome','STK11 (autosomal dominant)','hamartomatous polyps through the small bowel (intussusception), dark freckles on lips and buccal mucosa; raised risk of colon, breast, pancreas, stomach and ovarian cancer'],['Juvenile polyposis','SMAD4 or BMPR1A (autosomal dominant)','many hamartomatous juvenile polyps in children, rectal bleeding; raised cancer risk']]},
s:[['bio','Mismatch repair proteins (MSH2 with MSH6, MLH1 with PMS2) scan new DNA for mismatched bases and small loops after replication. Without them, short repeats (microsatellites) grow or shrink with every division, and frameshifts inactivate growth-control genes such as TGFBR2. Tumours are tested by immunohistochemistry for the four proteins or by microsatellite PCR.']]},
{h:'Colorectal cancer',p:[
'**Left-sided** cancers grow as napkin-ring strictures in a narrow colon carrying formed stool: change in bowel habit, thin stools, obstruction and visible blood. **Right-sided** cancers are exophytic masses in a wide colon carrying liquid stool: they bleed slowly and silently, so iron-deficiency anaemia and weight loss may be the only signs. Iron deficiency in an older man or a postmenopausal woman is colon cancer until proven otherwise.',
'Risk: age, family history, inflammatory bowel disease, red and processed meat, low fibre, smoking, alcohol, obesity and the inherited syndromes. Screening of average-risk adults from age 45 (colonoscopy every 10 years, or a yearly faecal immunochemical test). Spread is by lymphatics to regional nodes, by the portal vein to the liver (the commonest metastatic site) and, from the low rectum, by systemic veins to the lungs. Stage (depth of invasion, nodes, metastases) sets the prognosis. CEA is used to follow treated disease, not to screen.'],
s:[['pharm','5-fluorouracil inhibits thymidylate synthase (leucovorin strengthens the block); FOLFOX adds oxaliplatin (cold-triggered neuropathy) and FOLFIRI adds irinotecan (topoisomerase I; diarrhoea, worse with low UGT1A1). Cetuximab and panitumumab (anti-EGFR) work only in RAS wild-type tumours; bevacizumab blocks VEGF (bleeding, poor wound healing); pembrolizumab for MSI-high tumours. Aspirin modestly lowers colorectal cancer risk.'],['micro','Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) endocarditis and Clostridium septicum myonecrosis both call for a colonoscopy, because both are linked to a hidden colon cancer. Anal squamous cancer follows HPV 16 and 18, especially with HIV.']]},
{h:'Upper GI and liver cancers',p:[
'The upper tube has two oesophageal cancers with opposite stories. **Squamous cell carcinoma** grows in the upper and middle thirds after smoking, alcohol, very hot drinks, nitrosamines, achalasia, caustic injury and Plummer-Vinson syndrome; it is the commonest type worldwide. **Adenocarcinoma** grows in the lower third from Barrett oesophagus after years of reflux and obesity; it is the commonest type in Western countries.',
'**Gastric adenocarcinoma** comes in an intestinal type (H. pylori, atrophic gastritis, nitrosamines in smoked and salted food; gland-forming, ulcerated masses on the lesser curvature) and a diffuse type (loss of E-cadherin, CDH1; signet-ring cells infiltrating singly and stiffening the wall: **linitis plastica**). Both spread to the left supraclavicular node (Virchow), the ovaries (Krukenberg tumour, signet-ring cells), the umbilicus (Sister Mary Joseph nodule) and the pelvic peritoneum. **Acanthosis nigricans** and seborrhoeic keratoses that erupt suddenly (Leser-Trélat sign) can herald it.',
'**Carcinoid (well-differentiated neuroendocrine) tumours** arise mostly in the small bowel (ileum) and appendix and secrete serotonin. Serotonin from a gut primary drains into the portal vein and is destroyed by liver monoamine oxidase, so **carcinoid syndrome** (flushing, diarrhoea, wheeze, right-sided valve fibrosis) usually appears only with liver metastases; bronchial and ovarian carcinoids can cause it without, because they drain outside the portal system. Urinary 5-HIAA is raised. Diverting tryptophan into serotonin can cause pellagra (niacin deficiency).'],
table:{cols:['Tumour','Key risk','Clue'],rows:[
['Hepatocellular carcinoma','cirrhosis of any cause, hepatitis B (even without cirrhosis) and C, aflatoxin, haemochromatosis, α₁-antitrypsin deficiency','rising AFP; spreads into the portal and hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari); screen cirrhotic patients by ultrasound every 6 months'],
['Haemangioma','none','the commonest benign liver tumour; a blood-filled mass that should not be biopsied'],
['Hepatic adenoma','oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids','benign, but can rupture and bleed, especially in pregnancy; regresses when the hormone stops'],
['Angiosarcoma','vinyl chloride, arsenic, Thorotrast','malignant vascular tumour; CD31 positive'],
['Cholangiocarcinoma','primary sclerosing cholangitis, liver flukes (Clonorchis), choledochal cyst','obstructive jaundice; a hilar (Klatskin) tumour gives a small, impalpable gallbladder'],
['Metastases','colon, breast, lung, pancreas','the commonest liver masses of all'],
['Gastric MALT lymphoma','H. pylori','marginal-zone B-cell lymphoma that often regresses when H. pylori is eradicated'],
['Gastrointestinal stromal tumour (GIST)','none','interstitial cells of Cajal; KIT (CD117) or PDGFRA activating mutations; stomach most; imatinib']]},
s:[['micro','Hepatitis B can cause hepatocellular carcinoma without cirrhosis because its DNA integrates into the host genome and its HBx protein disrupts p53 and growth control. Aflatoxin from Aspergillus on stored grain and nuts causes a characteristic TP53 codon 249 mutation.'],['pharm','Carcinoid syndrome: octreotide or lanreotide (somatostatin analogues) block hormone release; telotristat inhibits tryptophan hydroxylase and reduces diarrhoea. Imatinib (a tyrosine kinase inhibitor) controls most GISTs. Sorafenib and immunotherapy combinations (atezolizumab with bevacizumab) treat advanced hepatocellular carcinoma.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 15-year-old has hundreds of colonic adenomas and osteomas of the jaw. Which gene is mutated, the same one whose loss starts most sporadic adenomas?',o:['KRAS','TP53','APC','BRAF'],a:2,d:'path',c:'colorectal-cancer',x:'Familial adenomatous polyposis with osteomas (the Gardner variant) is a germline APC mutation; APC normally helps destroy β-catenin, so its loss lets Wnt signalling drive crypt growth, the first step of the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. KRAS activation follows as the adenoma grows, and TP53 loss comes last, at the turn to carcinoma.'},
{q:'A 68-year-old woman has iron-deficiency anaemia and no symptoms. Most important next test?',o:['Colonoscopy','Bone marrow biopsy','Repeat iron studies','Trial of oral iron alone'],a:0,d:'path',c:'colorectal-cancer',x:'Right-sided (caecal) cancers are exophytic and bleed slowly into liquid stool without changing bowel habit, so iron deficiency may be the only sign. In an older adult it means gut blood loss until proven otherwise, so the colon (and usually the stomach) is examined. Treating with iron alone would hide the cause.'},
{q:'A 38-year-old has early satiety; endoscopy shows a thick, rigid stomach that does not distend, and biopsy shows signet-ring cells infiltrating singly. Mutated gene?',o:['MLH1 (mismatch repair)','APC (β-catenin control)','CDH1 (E-cadherin)','KRAS (GTPase switch)'],a:2,d:'path',c:'oncogenes',x:'This is diffuse-type gastric cancer (linitis plastica): loss of E-cadherin frees cells from one another, so they infiltrate the wall singly and provoke a stiff desmoplastic reaction; germline CDH1 mutations also cause lobular breast cancer. The intestinal type, linked to H. pylori, forms glands and ulcerated masses instead.'},
{q:'An ileal tumour causes flushing, diarrhoea, wheeze and tricuspid regurgitation. Where must it have spread?',o:['Lung','Liver','Bone','Brain'],a:1,d:'path',x:'Serotonin from a midgut carcinoid drains into the portal vein and is broken down by liver monoamine oxidase, so the syndrome appears only when liver metastases release it straight into the hepatic veins. It fibroses the tricuspid and pulmonary valves, then is inactivated in the lung, which spares the left heart. Bronchial and ovarian primaries can cause the syndrome without liver spread, but this tumour is ileal.'},
{q:'A 60-year-old obese man with years of heartburn develops progressive dysphagia, first to solids. Most likely tumour?',o:['Squamous cell carcinoma, upper third','Small cell carcinoma, middle third','Leiomyoma, lower third','Adenocarcinoma, lower third'],a:3,d:'path',x:'Chronic reflux replaces squamous epithelium with intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells (Barrett oesophagus), which progresses through dysplasia to adenocarcinoma of the lower third, now the commonest oesophageal cancer in Western countries. Squamous carcinoma of the upper and middle thirds follows smoking, alcohol and very hot drinks and is the commonest worldwide.'},
{q:'A 42-year-old has a right-sided mucinous colon cancer; his mother had endometrial cancer at 48. The tumour has lost MSH2 and is microsatellite-instability high. If it spreads, which drug is most likely to help?',o:['Cetuximab','Cyclophosphamide','Pembrolizumab','Imatinib'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'dna-repair',x:'Mismatch-repair loss (Lynch syndrome here) leaves thousands of frameshift mutations, so the tumour displays many neoantigens and responds well to PD-1 blockade with pembrolizumab. Cetuximab (anti-EGFR) helps only RAS wild-type tumours and is not chosen for MSI status, and imatinib targets KIT in GIST.'}
]};
